From 86b862fb2b3163945217c1e49a218a38d66a6184 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Andrei V. Lepikhov" <lepihov@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 06:59:56 +0200
Subject: [PATCH v0 04/10] Document why numerics must be unpacked, and cover
 the fallback path

The stack-unpacking helper added earlier explained what it does but not why a
conversion is needed at all, which left the interesting constraint implicit.
There are two independent obstacles, and either alone would force a copy.
NUMERIC_HEADER_SIZE() and NUMERIC_DIGITS() are defined in terms of VARHDRSZ, so
on a short-header value every field sits three bytes from where the accessors
look for it -- the result is not a misaligned read but the wrong bytes.  And
heap_fill_tuple() stores a convertible varlena in short form with no alignment
padding at all, so even accessors taught about the short layout would be doing
unaligned 16-bit reads.  Spell both out, so that the next person to wonder
whether the copy can be skipped has the answer in front of them.

The comment on the bound test claimed the test was unwanted and quoted a
figure for removing it.  The figure was real but not general: whether a bounded
copy is expanded inline or turned into a memcpy call is a code-generation
decision, and compilers do not agree about it.  State the portable reason for
keeping the test instead -- it is the only thing bounding a memcpy whose length
comes from on-disk data, and the reasoning that makes it unfailable spans three
constants in two files, which is too thin a thread for an unchecked write to a
fixed stack buffer.

Give numeric_eq()'s byte-equality shortcut its argument rather than its
conclusion: everything past the varlena header is the numeric's own header word
followed by the digit array, so equal length plus equal bytes means equal sign,
weight, dscale and digits, specials included.  Note also that inspecting the
toast flags before detoasting is deliberate and safe, since those macros read
only the first header byte -- the obvious "fix" would reintroduce exactly the
detoast the shortcut avoids.

Finally, add regression coverage for the fallback.  Nothing in numeric.sql
hashed, grouped, sorted or compared a value wide enough to be stored out of
line, so the DatumGetNumeric() arm of the helper had no test at all and could
have been broken without any failure showing up.
